From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@local>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 17:29:08 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] kk: query pools past Metal's per-buffer and per-process
 limits

Every occlusion query of a device lived in one Metal visibility result
buffer of 32768 entries (Metal's maximum visibility query offset is 256 KB),
so vkCreateQueryPool failed once a device had 32768 live occlusion queries.
Timestamp pools took Metal counter heaps of their own, and Metal allows 32
counter heaps per process ("Maximum number of sample buffers"; newCounterHeap
fails with "too many heaps already allocated in this process or on the
system"). Under virglrenderer every guest application's device lives in the
VMM process, so the 33rd timestamp pool of the whole VM failed. Venus
creates pools asynchronously: the failed pool became an unknown object and
the guest's next vkCmdBeginQuery / vkCmdResetQueryPool on it was a CS error
that killed its GPU context (Dota 2 and Counter-Strike 2 on M3/M4/M5).

Occlusion results now live in chunks of 32768 created as pools need them
(up to 16 per device, 524288 queries); a pool of up to one chunk takes its
queries from a single chunk. Pools keep 32-bit indices and libkk's reset and
copy kernels find a chunk through the device's chunk table. A render pass
writes visibility results to the chunk of its active query; beginning a query
of another chunk restarts the Metal render pass with that chunk.

Timestamp pools take chunks of 32 entries of counter heaps of 4096 created
once for the process and shared by all devices (up to 32 heaps, 131072
entries), instead of heaps of their own.
